※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A value in brackets may be written down the page.
※
※ **No end of line while a bracket is open.**  The lexer counts them, and gives
※ out no end of statement while any is open, so a call, a parameter list, an
※ array, a tuple, a set or a dictionary may be written over as many lines as it
※ takes.  What ends a statement is a line break the reader can see is one, and a
※ break inside brackets is not that: the bracket says the value goes on.
※
※ **A block written on one line ends at a closing bracket.**  `f(if c: 1u8 else:
※ 2u8)` is a block opened by the colon and closed by the `)`, which is what the
※ parser does with it: it reads the statements of the block until what comes next
※ cannot continue one, and a closing bracket cannot.  A comma ends one the same
※ way, where the block is one value of several.
※
※ This is the file the tree-sitter grammar is held to as well: the test that
※ compares the two reads every program here, so a shape the compiler takes and
※ the grammar refuses is a failing test rather than a squiggle in an editor.
※
※ Compare: **Python**, whose brackets suppress its own newlines by the same rule
※ and where a backslash is what continues a line outside them; **C** and
※ **JavaScript**, which have no layout to suppress; **Haskell**, whose layout
※ rule also stops inside brackets.

fn wide(a: u8,
        b: u8,
        c: u8) → u8:
    a + b + c

fn pick(a: u8, b: u8) → u8:
    a + b

@[startup,
  impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ a call down the page
    let sum: u8 = wide(1u8,
                       2u8,
                       3u8)
    ※ an array, with the elements where they read best
    let v: u8⟦4⟧ = ⟦1u8, 2u8,
                    3u8, 4u8⟧
    ※ a set whose closing bracket is on a line of its own, and at a column no
    ※ block would allow
    let s: ⸨u8⸩ = ⸨1u8,
2u8
⸩
    ※ and a dictionary written the way one is read
    let d: mut ⸨str: u8⸩ = ⸨
        "a": 1u8,
        "b": 2u8⸩
    if sum ≠ 6u8:
        1u6
    elif v⟦3⟧ ≠ 4u8:
        2u6
    elif ¬s⸨2u8⸩:
        3u6
    elif (d⸨"b"⸩ ?? 0u8) ≠ 2u8:
        4u6
    else:
        blocks()

※ A block written on one line, inside brackets.
@[impure]
fn blocks() → u6:
    ※ closed by the bracket that closes the call
    let one: u8 = pick(if 1u8 = 1u8: 1u8 else: 2u8,
                       ※ and by the comma that follows the first
                       if 1u8 = 2u8: 4u8 else: 8u8)
    ※ and by the bracket of a value written down
    let two: u8⟦2⟧ = ⟦if 1u8 = 1u8: 1u8 else: 2u8, 3u8⟧
    ※ a lambda whose body is written on the line, inside a call
    let three: u8 = calling(λx: u8 → u8: x + 1u8)
    if one ≠ 9u8:
        5u6
    elif two⟦0⟧ ≠ 1u8:
        6u6
    elif three ≠ 2u8:
        7u6
    else:
        0u6

@[impure]
fn calling(f: fn(u8) → u8) → u8:
    f(1u8)
